---
import {THEME_TOGGLE} from "../content/site";

// The icon is chosen in CSS from the `data-theme` attribute that the inline script
// in BasicLayout sets before first paint — so it never flashes wrong.
//
// The button reports which theme is active through `aria-pressed`, and its name
// stays the same in both themes. That pairing is not a free choice: WAI-ARIA's
// toggle-button guidance says the name of a control carrying a pressed state must
// NOT change with the state, and offers a changing name as the alternative *instead
// of* the state. Doing both is what its own text warns against, so this file picks
// one and `THEME_TOGGLE.name` in `src/content/site.ts` is written to be state-independent.
//
// A changing name was the other candidate and it is fully sanctioned — it would also
// need no JS here, since CSS could swap two screen-reader-only strings off the same
// attribute the icons use. It was rejected on measurement, not taste: Sarah Higley's
// screen-reader survey found a name change announced in only about half of
// reader/browser combinations while `aria-pressed` was announced in all of them, and
// concluded "use state for all other toggle buttons" — everything except play/pause.
// Since the entire point of this markup is that activating the button says
// something, the reliably-announced mechanism wins. Announcement on activation is
// the whole benefit; being correct when the button is merely re-read is the floor,
// and both options cleared it.
//
// What was there before did neither. `aria-live="polite"` sat on this button, and it
// could not fire: everything inside that changes on activation is decorative, and
// the one text node never changed. It read as state exposure while providing none.
//
// The pressed state is written by the script below rather than trusted from the
// server, and that ordering is the one sharp edge. This button does not exist yet
// when the pre-paint script runs, so the server's `aria-pressed` can only describe
// the default theme; a visitor who prefers dark would otherwise be handed a button
// asserting the opposite. The module script is deferred, so it corrects the
// attribute after the document parses and before anyone can reach the button, and
// with no JS at all the theme cannot change either, so the server's value stays
// true. Keep the initial attribute agreeing with the `data-theme` the layout
// renders — a test pins that pair.
//
// This is the page's only true button: it runs a handler and has no URL. It used to
// wear a second, narrower variant of the box beside it, which is what made it the odd
// one out; that variant no longer exists, so nothing here may reintroduce a box metric
// of its own. Name no retired class in this comment either — .astro frontmatter IS
// scanned for class names, so a token written here alone emits a rule with no wearer
// and the orphan-rule test fails the build.
//
// THE BOX IS NO LONGER A CHOICE, AND THE PROP THAT USED TO MAKE IT ONE IS GONE. There
// were two wearers and two answers: the intro card drew this among plated social links
// and a page header draws it among chips. The plate is the mark for a page's ONE action,
// so the intro card's row was that mark spent on furniture and it is a strip of quiet
// glyph boxes now — which leaves both consumers passing the same value, i.e. a prop with
// one answer and a default naming a class the sheet no longer has. One box, declared
// once, in the shortcut.
//
// IT IS THE GLYPH BOX AND NOT THE LABELLED ONE. This control holds one mark and its
// name is screen-reader-only; the labelled chip is for a control that names itself in a
// word, and putting this in one would render it about 36px wide with its glyph shrunk
// to a label's text size.
//
// A DRAWN LABEL WAS CONSIDERED AND IS NOT AVAILABLE HERE. SC 2.5.3 (Label in Name)
// requires the accessible name to contain whatever label a reader can see, and the
// accessible name is `THEME_TOGGLE.name` — deliberately naming the THEME the pressed
// state refers to, so the polarity cannot reverse unnoticed. A chip drawn with that
// word beside a sun glyph in light mode says two different things. The idea worth
// revisiting is to fix the glyph, label it with that same string so the drawn and
// accessible names are ONE string, and let the chip's own reversed state carry
// `aria-pressed="true"` — which would make this control's state perceivable for the
// first time. That costs a mark from the census and a rewrite of the glyph-swapping
// style below; it is a decision about this control rather than about where it sits.
---

<button
        id="theme-toggle"
        type="button"
        aria-pressed="false"
        class="theme-toggle chip-icon"
>
    <span class="i-ri-sun-line theme-icon-light shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="i-ri-moon-line theme-icon-dark shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="sr-only">{THEME_TOGGLE.name}</span>
</button>

<style is:global>
    /* data-theme is always "light" or "dark" (set pre-paint in BasicLayout);
       the descendant selector outranks presetIcons' display:inline-block. */
    .theme-toggle .theme-icon-dark {
        display: none;
    }

    :root[data-theme="dark"] .theme-toggle .theme-icon-light {
        display: none;
    }

    :root[data-theme="dark"] .theme-toggle .theme-icon-dark {
        display: inline-block;
    }

    /*
     * THE TOGGLE'S GLYPH SURVIVES FORCED COLOURS, by the shared rule in BasicLayout.astro.
     * This control carries no words on screen at all, so losing the glyph loses the control
     * entirely — the sun and moon painted rgb(255,255,255) on a rgb(255,255,255) card, 1.00:1,
     * gone. It is the one control here that takes `ButtonText` rather than `LinkText`: it
     * changes the page rather than leaving it, and the mode reserves a colour for each. That
     * distinction is drawn by what CONTAINS the mark, so this button gets it by being a
     * button, with nothing to declare here.
     */

</style>

<script>
    const toggle = document.getElementById("theme-toggle");
    if (toggle) {
        const root = document.documentElement;
        // Reports the state rather than assuming it, so the first call also
        // corrects the server's guess for a visitor whose stored or system
        // preference is dark.
        const report = () => toggle.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(root.dataset.theme === "dark"));
        report();
        toggle.addEventListener("click", () => {
            const next = root.dataset.theme === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
            root.dataset.theme = next;
            localStorage.setItem("theme", next);
            report();
        });
    }
</script>
